---
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Ascii from '../components/Ascii.astro';
import { getNotes } from '../lib/site';

const notes = await getNotes();
const pictures = notes.map((n) => n.data.ascii.art ?? n.data.ascii.piece);
const pieces = new Set(pictures);
const generated = notes.filter((n) => n.data.ascii.art).length;
const tags = new Set(notes.flatMap((n) => n.data.tags));
---
<BaseLayout title="Colophon" description="How this site is built, how it is dressed, and how the privacy gate works.">
  <div class="window window-shadow" style="max-width: 62rem; margin-inline: auto;">
    <div class="title-bar title-bar--active">
      <span class="close-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <h1 class="title">Colophon</h1>
      <span class="zoom-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="window-body">
      <div class="prose">
        <Ascii piece="file-tree" caption="the whole site is a directory of markdown files" />

        <p>How this notebook is put together, in case any of it is worth copying.</p>

        <h2>The desktop</h2>
        <p>
          The site is dressed as a System 7 Macintosh: a dithered screen, white windows
          with pinstriped title bars, close and zoom boxes on every window, and a menu bar
          with the rainbow Apple at its left. The front page is a Finder desktop — the
          windows and the icons on it can be dragged around, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> puts them
          back — and every other page is a document window on the same screen, with an
          <b>Info</b> window beside it for the things a rail would normally hold.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is a one-bit design on purpose: there is one ink and one paper, and every grey
          on the page is a <b>dither pattern</b> — a 50% or 25% checkerboard at one pixel
          per cell — because that is how a one-bit screen made grey. The only colour on the
          site is the Apple in the menu bar, which is the one place System 7 spent it
          deliberately, on the rainbow logo.
        </p>

        <h2>Inverted video</h2>
        <p>
          <b>Invert</b> in the menu bar swaps ink and paper: a black desktop, white ink,
          and the ascii pictures rendered as negatives, which is the same trick as the
          Mac's own inverted-video switch. The choice is kept in local storage, and a
          reader whose operating system asks for dark mode starts inverted. Turn it off and
          you are back on paper.
        </p>

        <h2>The type</h2>
        <p>
          Four faces, all self-hosted, so no font service ever learns that a page was read:
          <b>Chicago</b> for the chrome — menus, window titles, buttons and headings —
          <b>Geneva</b> for small labels and captions, <b>Monaco</b> for the prose and the
          code, because this is a notes site and its voice is monospace, and Chicago again,
          as an outline face, for the wordmark. Chicago 12 and Geneva 9 are recreations by
          Giles Booth; all four files come from
          <a href="https://github.com/sakofchit/system.css" rel="noopener noreferrer">system.css</a>
          by <a href="https://github.com/sakofchit" rel="noopener noreferrer">@sakofchit</a>
          (MIT), the same licence the ascii library is under.
        </p>

        <h2>Building</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Astro</b>, static output. No server runtime, no database, no client-side router.</li>
          <li>Notes are Markdown in <code>src/content/notes/</code>, read through a content collection with a typed frontmatter schema. A bad field fails the build.</li>
          <li>The frontmatter names the ascii piece; the name is checked against the library catalogue at build time, so a typo is a build error instead of an empty frame.</li>
          <li>Dates are formatted with an explicit <code>timeZone: 'UTC'</code> so two builds of the same commit produce identical HTML.</li>
          <li>The client-side JavaScript is the theme switch, the menu-bar clock, the ascii runtime and the desktop's dragging. Nothing else ships.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The art</h2>
        <p>
          Each note carries a picture: a photograph decoded onto a character grid, produced by
          <code>tools/make-art.py</code>. That script searches
          <a href="https://wallhaven.cc/" rel="noopener noreferrer">wallhaven</a> for one image
          per note, centre-crops it to the grid's aspect, downsamples it to
          <b>132 × 37 cells</b>, maps each cell's luminance through a 70-character density ramp
          for the glyphs, and median-cuts the colours down to a 64-entry palette that travels
          with the module. The frames are not stored: the finished picture is the data, and a
          deterministic hash decides which cells have decoded yet, so the piece plays a short
          decode reveal and then holds.
        </p>
        <p>
          {generated} of the {pieces.size} pictures are generated that way; the rest are
          schematic pieces from <a href="https://ascii.rest/" rel="noopener noreferrer">ascii.rest</a>
          by <a href="https://github.com/bas3line" rel="noopener noreferrer">@bas3line</a> (MIT),
          which is also what animates a generated piece on a canvas — it handles the device
          pixel ratio, the colour fills and the resize. Nothing plays off screen. On the page
          the canvas is filtered down to ink, because a one-bit desktop will not carry a
          colour photograph; the colour is still in the module, where the decoder put it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three readers, three experiences, all from one module: with JavaScript off the finished
          picture is already in the HTML as plain text; with reduced motion asked for, that same
          still is what you keep and the piece is never mounted; everyone else gets the decode
          on a canvas. The <a href={url('/art/')}>art page</a> has all {pieces.size} of them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The photographs are other people's work, found through a search API. Republishing a
          derivative of someone's image needs thought, so each caption links the source and the
          generator records the image id, URL and query in the module header and in
          <code>src/art/sources.js</code>. Check the terms of anything you intend to publish.
        </p>

        <h2>The privacy gate</h2>
        <p>
          <code>scripts/check-anonymized.mjs</code> walks the sources, the public assets and
          the built HTML and fails on:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>email addresses and phone-number-shaped strings;</li>
          <li>IPv4 addresses, except documentation ranges (<code>10.x</code>, <code>192.0.2.x</code>, <code>127.0.0.1</code>);</li>
          <li>absolute paths that contain a username — <code>/home/…</code>, <code>/Users/…</code>, <code>C:\Users\…</code>;</li>
          <li>credential shapes: <code>sk-…</code>, <code>ghp_…</code>, <code>AKIA…</code>, <code>Bearer …</code>, PEM headers;</li>
          <li>every term listed in <code>.private-terms</code>, a git-ignored file holding the
              names and identifiers that must never be published.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Keeping the private terms in a git-ignored file matters: a denylist committed to
          the repository would itself be the leak. The script reports how many terms it
          loaded, so an empty list is visible rather than silent.
        </p>

        <h2>Verification</h2>
        <p>
          <code>scripts/verify_site.py</code> starts a preview server, walks every route,
          checks for non-200s, missing titles and console errors, asserts that each note page
          actually contains a rendered ascii frame (a <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code> or a
          <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> with content, not just an empty container), that the
          desktop's pattern layer is painted, and that the Invert switch really flips the
          palette and survives a reload, and it writes screenshots as evidence. A page that
          merely returns 200 is not a page that works.
        </p>

        <p>
          Counts at the last build: {notes.length} notes, {tags.size} tags,
          {pieces.length} distinct ascii pieces.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
